s": {"margin": 13.05, "lean": 9.0, "environment": 4.15, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -0.1}, "rating_margin": 16.0, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 5.9, "baseline_margin": 17.2, "analyst_adjustment": 0.3, "key_factors": ["Becerra leads every recent poll by 10 to 26 points in a state that leans 12 points Democratic", "All seven major raters place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column", "Prediction markets price a Becerra win at roughly 96 percent", "Hilton's slight fundraising edge does little to offset California's structural Democratic advantage"], "rationale": "High-quality pollsters from PPIC to Berkeley IGS consistently show Becerra ahead by double digits, with the latest surveys indicating a widening lead, and every major rating service calls the race Solid or Safe Democratic. California's strong Democratic lean leaves Hilton an extremely narrow path to an upset despite an energetic campaign and modest fundraising advantage.", "watch": "A late scandal, a major Becerra stumble, or sharply uneven turnout in a low-salience open-seat race could narrow what polls show as a wide lead.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Gavin Newsom (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "California's open governor's race pits Democrat Xavier Becerra against Republican Steve Hilton to succeed term-limited Gavin Newsom. The state's strong Democratic lean gives Becerra a clear structural advantage, and polls throughout the fall have shown him leading by ten to twenty-six points, with the most recent surveys pointing to a widening margin. Every major race rating places the contest in the Solid or Safe Democratic column.\n\nHilton has run an energetic campaign built on voter discontent and cheeky advertising, and he has slightly out-raised Becerra, but he remains a heavy underdog in a state where no Republican has won statewide in decades. Betting markets price a Becerra victory at roughly 96 percent. The main uncertainty involves turnout in a quiet race and whethe